 "checked_text": "this page is a non-preemptive sjf timeline: gray means waiting, black means running. we'll answer your question by following each cpu choice, especially the p9-p8-p7 group and p12's long wait.\n\nat t=0, p1, p2, and p3 are all ready with burst 1. since they tie, listing order picks p1, then p2, then p3; their waits become 0, 1, and 2.\np1 wait 0\np2 wait 1\np3 wait 2\n\nat t=3, p4, p5, and p6 arrive together. sjf picks the shortest burst first: p4 runs 3-4, then p5 4-6, then p6 6-9.\np4 wait 0\np5 wait 1\np6 wait 3\n\nnow your main question: at t=9, p7, p8, and p9 are all ready. their bursts are 3, 2, and 1, so sjf chooses p9, then p8, then p7.\nburst 1 -> first\nburst 2 -> next\nburst 3 -> last\n\nbecause p9 runs 9-10, p8 runs 10-12, and p7 runs 12-15. waiting is start minus arrival, so their waits are 2, 3, and 5.\np9 wait 2\np8 wait 3\np7 wait 5\n\nat t=15, p10, p11, and p12 are ready, but p12 has burst 3. p10 and p11 are shorter, then at t=18 the new p13 and p14 are also shorter, so p12 finally runs 21-24.\nshorter: wait 2\nshorter: wait 3\np12 wait 8\n\nnotice the non-preemptive detail: p12 is waiting, not running, so new shorter jobs can still be chosen when the cpu frees up. p13 waits 1, p14 waits 2, and p12 waits until 21.\np13 wait 1\np14 wait 2\ndelay p12\n\nadd every wait: 0+1+2+0+1+3+5+3+2+2+3+8+1+2 = 33. divide by 14 processes: the average waiting time is 33/14 = 2.36 time units.\navg wait = 2.36\ncpu free -> find ready\nfind ready -> pick shortest burst\npick shortest burst -> run to finish\nrun to finish -> record wait\nrecord wait -> cpu free"
